Consider the equation of the chord. There are several ways. One is to make this a family of chords by the auxiliary circle and use inversion. Another is to adjust our choice of coordinate system by translating to make $c=0$, then add a repeating chord along $x=-c$ and use the second derivative to find families.


The chord forms a chord with the vertex....<answer> Let chord AB be $$y=k(x-c), k\neq 0 $$ that meets $$y=x^2$$ at $$x^2=k(x-c) \Rightarrow x^2-kx+ck=0$$ and this gives us points $$A(h, \ k(h-c)) \text{ and } B(l, \ kl(l-c)) \\ \begin{align*} AC^2 &=(h-0)^2+(k(h-c)-c)^2 \\ &=(h-k(h-c)-c)^2 \\ &=(hc+(1-k)h)^2 \text{ (1)} \\ BC^2 &=(l-0)^2+(kl(l-c)-c)^2 \\ &=(l-k(l-c)-c)^2 \\ &= (lc+(1-k)l)^2 \text{ (2)}\end{align*}\\ \begin{align*} \frac{1}{AC^2}+\frac{1}{BC^2}=\frac{1}{(h-k(h-c)-c)^2}+\frac{1}{(l-k(l-c)-c)^2} \\ =\frac{ch^2-lc^2+hk(h+1)-kc(1+k)+2kc^2}{(h-k(h-c)-c)^2} \text{ (3)} \\ \cdots \end{align*}
